# Expense Reimbursement Policy — accommodation

> Module Accommodation

> Using ExpenseDefs

E-4 legislates per night ("£220 per night"), so `NightlyAccommodation` answers
for one night. The module computes two things that E-4 keeps apart and that a
merged encoding would confuse: the **cap** that applies to the night (E-4.1 as
possibly uplifted by E-4.2) and the **quantity measured against that cap**
(E-4.3).

## Prologue — declarations

```catala-metadata
declaration scope NightlyAccommodation:
  input city_tier content ExpenseDefs.CityTier
  input room_cost content money
  input local_taxes content money
  input mandatory_resort_or_city_charge content money
  input parking_cost content money
  input no_compliant_accommodation_available_evidenced content boolean
  internal base_cap content money
  output applicable_cap content money
  output cost_against_cap content money
  output accommodation_reimbursable content money
  output parking_reimbursable content money
  output reimbursable_amount content money
```

`no_compliant_accommodation_available_evidenced` is the **judgement input** for
this module. E-4.2 is HYBRID: the relief it gives is exact arithmetic, but it is
triggered by the Employee providing evidence that no *compliant* accommodation
was *available* within the cap at the time of booking, and the Policy reduces
none of compliance, availability or evidential sufficiency to a test. The
assessment is therefore supplied to the scope by whoever made it, and this
module never infers it — not from `room_cost` exceeding the cap, not from
anything else.

## E-4.1 Nightly caps

| EXP-POL E-4.1 (002-expense-reimbursement-policy.md:69)
|
| Accommodation is reimbursable at actual cost up to £220 per night in a
| Tier 1 city, £160 per night in a Tier 2 city and £120 per night in a Tier 3
| city.

Two limbs. The table fixes the cap for the night, and "at actual cost up to"
fixes the reimbursable amount as the lesser of the cost and the cap. The table
is lifted into a toplevel function so that E-4.2 can multiply "the applicable
cap" without restating the figures.

`accommodation_reimbursable` is compared against `cost_against_cap`, which is
the composite quantity E-4.3 defines below — not against `room_cost`.

"At actual cost" is also a bound on the input. The room cost is the sum the
Employee actually paid for the night, so it is nought or more; a negative room
cost is not a cost E-4.1 could reimburse, and it is the clause's own words that
fix this rather than any programming convenience. Asserted here because E-4.1
is the clause that makes the actual cost operative.

```catala
declaration tier_night_cap content money
  depends on tier content ExpenseDefs.CityTier
  equals
    match tier with pattern
    -- Tier1: $220.00
    -- Tier2: $160.00
    -- Tier3: $120.00

scope NightlyAccommodation:
  assertion room_cost >= $0.00

  definition base_cap equals tier_night_cap of city_tier

  label e4_1 definition applicable_cap equals base_cap

  definition accommodation_reimbursable equals
    Money.min of cost_against_cap, applicable_cap
```

## E-4.2 No compliant accommodation within the cap

| EXP-POL E-4.2 (002-expense-reimbursement-policy.md:73)
|
| By way of exception to E-4.1, where the Employee provides evidence that
| no compliant accommodation was available within the applicable cap at the time
| of booking, the actual cost is reimbursable up to 1.4 times the applicable cap.

An exception to `e4_1` on `applicable_cap`. The parent definition is
unconditional — E-4.1's cap applies to every night — so there is no parent
condition to repeat in the exception.

The relief changes the cap and nothing else: the comparison is still against
`cost_against_cap`, so a night whose actual cost exceeds 1.4 times the cap is
still reimbursed only to 1.4 times the cap. "Up to 1.4 times" is a ceiling, not
a substitution of actual cost.

```catala
```

## E-4.3 What the cap is measured against

| EXP-POL E-4.3 (002-expense-reimbursement-policy.md:77)
|
| The caps in E-4.1 are inclusive of local taxes and of any mandatory
| resort or city charge, and exclusive of the cost of parking.

E-4.3 is a computation rule, not a gloss: without it the E-4.1 comparison has
no defined left-hand side. Local taxes and any mandatory resort or city charge
are inside the cap, so they are added to the room cost before the comparison —
a £210 room in a Tier 1 city with £25 of city charge is £235 against a £220 cap
and is reimbursed at £220, not £210. Parking is outside the cap, so it is
excluded from that sum.

| NOTE: E-4.3 settles what the cap measures; it does not itself say that parking
| is reimbursable. This module adopts the implication that it is — reimbursed
| alongside the capped accommodation cost rather than against it — because
| excluding parking from the cap while denying it altogether would leave the
| exclusion doing no work. The alternative reading is that parking is simply
| outside E-4 and falls to be dealt with under E-1.2. The two outputs are kept
| separate (`accommodation_reimbursable`, `parking_reimbursable`) so that a
| caller taking the narrower view can read the capped figure alone.

**Each of the three sums E-4.3 sorts is nought or more, and the clause is what
fixes that.** E-4.3 names "local taxes", "any mandatory resort or city charge"
and "the cost of parking": a tax, a mandatory charge and a cost are each an
amount levied or paid, and none of them can be a negative amount. The bounds
are not conveniences, because each of the three does specific work under this
clause — the first two are added into the quantity measured against the cap and
the third is kept out of it — and a negative figure would make that work run
backwards. Nothing in E-4 contemplates a credit, a refund or a rebate; a night
that cost less is a smaller cost, not a negative one.

The parking bound is the load-bearing one. Parking sits *outside* the cap, so
it is added to the capped accommodation figure rather than measured against it,
and a negative parking cost was therefore able to pull the reimbursable total
below the cap the module had itself computed under E-4.1 — a Tier 1 room of
£400 with parking of -£100 was reimbursed at £120 against a £220 cap. Recorded
by the adversarial reviewer as the unguarded-input-domain class of
`tests/counterexamples/CE-0011.yaml` rather than as a counterexample of its
own, because no prose fact pattern states a cost in that form.

```catala
scope NightlyAccommodation:
  assertion local_taxes >= $0.00

  assertion mandatory_resort_or_city_charge >= $0.00

  assertion parking_cost >= $0.00

  definition cost_against_cap equals
    room_cost + local_taxes + mandatory_resort_or_city_charge

  definition parking_reimbursable equals parking_cost

  definition reimbursable_amount equals
    accommodation_reimbursable + parking_reimbursable
```
